  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 328 PM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nVery hot and smoky weather continues. Public report from \nWilliston indicated smoky conditions with the smell of smoke in\nthe air. The latest visibility observation from Williston has \ndropped to 4SM. Latest satellite imagery shows smoke rolling in \nfrom Montana. There is a rather sharp cutoff just north of the \nInternational border, with much less smoke in Canada. Upper \nlevel winds are keeping most of the smoke confined to the \nContinental U.S and there is a definite thickening of the smoke \nalong the International Border. It remains smoky as you go \nsouth, but smoke concentration is less. Have expanded the smoke\nto include much of western and central ND.\n\nThe Heat Advisory remains in effect through 9 PM CDT this\nevening. There were considerations of expanding this through\ntomorrow in the southwest but in the end we decided to hold off\non this. Forecast lows tonight are generally in the low to mid \n60s, while last night lows were in the upper 60s to lower 70s. \nAlthough not cool by any stretch of the imagination, the lower \ntemperatures will limit the overnight heat impacts. There are \nsome differences in forecast highs tomorrow, and in the end, \nfelt there was too much uncertainty to continue the Advisory. We\nstill may need to issue something overnight for the far \nsouthwest and/or south central, but will hold off for now. \nForecast highs Monday are generally in the 90s. A few 80s may be\nfound in the far north, and a few readings of 100 to 103 are \npossible in the southwest and south central, generally along \nand south of the I-94 corridor. \n\nThe NBM has been carrying a low chance (around 20 percent)\nprobability for thunderstorms tomorrow, generally in the \nsouthwest and south central. Highs are around 100 degrees and \nthere will likely be some capping issues, but there could be \nsome moderate instability during the max heating of the day \nwithin an area where shortwave energy is propagating through the\nupper level ridge. Better probabilities remain south of the \nforecast area, but given the heat, and for consistency will \nmaintain these pops.\n\nThe remainder of the workweek looks to be not quite as hot as\nthis weekend, but we still remain 10 to 15 degrees above normal\n(normal is in the lower 80s) in most areas. With the hot \ntemperatures and impulses propagating through the mean upper \nridge, generally low pops (20 to 30%) are anticipated through \nthe week. A more vigorous wave does move through the mean flow \nin the Wednesday night through Thursday timeframe. Currently the\nNBM is depicting a broadbrush area of 30 to 60 percent pops. We\nwill see if these pops remain that high, but the large \nuncertainty in timing and placement does necessitate the \nbroadbrush nature of the pops at this time.",
